The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset = -1;
```
```c
c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset = eax & 0xff;
```
```c
if (c->x86_vendor == X86_VENDOR_INTEL)
    c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset = eax & 0xff;
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code hardcodes the memory bandwidth monitoring counter's width to 24 bits for AMD processors by setting `c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset` to `-1` initially and not adjusting it correctly based on AMD's capabilities. This leads to an inadequate measurement limit of 1GB/s, causing overflow issues for higher bandwidths.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset = eax & 0xff;
```
```c
if (c->x86_vendor == X86_VENDOR_AMD && !c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset)
    c->x86_cache_mbm_width_offset = MBM_CNTR_WIDTH_OFFSET_AMD;
```
Reason:
The fix involves correctly setting the memory bandwidth monitoring counter's width for AMD processors by checking if the vendor is AMD and if the width offset is not already set. If not, it assigns the appropriate wider counter width, allowing the software to measure bandwidth beyond 1GB/s without overflow.

